[[:db/retract 17592186115850 :question/answer-fields 17592186114037] [:db/retract 17592186115731 :question/answer-fields 17592186113352] {:db/id 17592186115251 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b47d0cc7-71aa-58ba-a792-0650346f33fc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115080 :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2 \\,$ and $\\, R_2 :=  R_2+(-1)R_1?$\n\n![](images/e0/e034f65af225ce46015a5accae774a24eed8617590a583a2a885b4b1c5332103.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115250 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bcd8ebdd-840f-56b3-b01b-b55941f100d8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115079 :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n![](images/91/919674aa42fd55c536318518d9e14de07a515d3c33cb7868378ce168221f13ff.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115078 :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\nR_1 := \\dfrac{1}{2}R_1,\n\\qquad\nR_1 := R_1+(-3)R_2.\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n![](images/b3/b34f8d0d2c5f05f6c104395e75e9f5c93df7812155e09bc47828747e752d003a.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b47d0cc7-71aa-58ba-a792-0650346f33fc" :question/id #uuid "b47d0cc7-71aa-58ba-a792-0650346f33fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-75505" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f7d95426-738e-5629-870c-65da360e7800"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f7d95426-738e-5629-870c-65da360e7800" :answer-field/id #uuid "f7d95426-738e-5629-870c-65da360e7800" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-05827898-9cd0-5481-9f4f-2795de94047f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-41d70487-8623-57c9-b21d-be91769cf161" "answer-5daf2709-08dc-5216-87eb-2c674ea61e85" "answer-0fb31cbb-95b7-5502-b5a4-6f06608e01c1" "answer-05827898-9cd0-5481-9f4f-2795de94047f" "answer-932f9073-db7f-59db-843a-495c4d7ae846"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41d70487-8623-57c9-b21d-be91769cf161" :answer/id #uuid "41d70487-8623-57c9-b21d-be91769cf161" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5daf2709-08dc-5216-87eb-2c674ea61e85" :answer/id #uuid "5daf2709-08dc-5216-87eb-2c674ea61e85" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0fb31cbb-95b7-5502-b5a4-6f06608e01c1" :answer/id #uuid "0fb31cbb-95b7-5502-b5a4-6f06608e01c1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05827898-9cd0-5481-9f4f-2795de94047f" :answer/id #uuid "05827898-9cd0-5481-9f4f-2795de94047f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-932f9073-db7f-59db-843a-495c4d7ae846" :answer/id #uuid "932f9073-db7f-59db-843a-495c4d7ae846" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115850 :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${A}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${A}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c76094cd-e064-540b-baa0-d39b44e34908"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c76094cd-e064-540b-baa0-d39b44e34908" :answer-field/id #uuid "c76094cd-e064-540b-baa0-d39b44e34908" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-482e772e-15aa-5a49-ba61-a3210d49dce1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-30ffe217-9ca4-5886-8537-db99704507c9" "answer-27195729-1b10-59a4-9a7d-c8f3fb5b730d" "answer-693517ab-7974-5643-b63c-0b1e93f9bf86" "answer-f90d54bb-bb78-5ccd-89f4-75dfe86ac00f" "answer-482e772e-15aa-5a49-ba61-a3210d49dce1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-30ffe217-9ca4-5886-8537-db99704507c9" :answer/id #uuid "30ffe217-9ca4-5886-8537-db99704507c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27195729-1b10-59a4-9a7d-c8f3fb5b730d" :answer/id #uuid "27195729-1b10-59a4-9a7d-c8f3fb5b730d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-693517ab-7974-5643-b63c-0b1e93f9bf86" :answer/id #uuid "693517ab-7974-5643-b63c-0b1e93f9bf86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f90d54bb-bb78-5ccd-89f4-75dfe86ac00f" :answer/id #uuid "f90d54bb-bb78-5ccd-89f4-75dfe86ac00f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-482e772e-15aa-5a49-ba61-a3210d49dce1" :answer/id #uuid "482e772e-15aa-5a49-ba61-a3210d49dce1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bcd8ebdd-840f-56b3-b01b-b55941f100d8" :question/id #uuid "bcd8ebdd-840f-56b3-b01b-b55941f100d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-131383" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $D$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${D}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $D$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $D$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${D}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7e6a2e19-fc40-5f23-8312-fc7a5a3459ae"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7e6a2e19-fc40-5f23-8312-fc7a5a3459ae" :answer-field/id #uuid "7e6a2e19-fc40-5f23-8312-fc7a5a3459ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-36ed8b6e-c128-5d01-928b-08f774f8d809" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8dbc9696-4140-550a-b72f-debd09d5fe0d" "answer-351e7f30-9491-58fc-8cf5-793274bcf323" "answer-f8aa4dff-702b-54c5-9a1b-1549247cc0ac" "answer-36ed8b6e-c128-5d01-928b-08f774f8d809" "answer-7e84734a-f76d-51fa-a6d7-e244eb448774"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8dbc9696-4140-550a-b72f-debd09d5fe0d" :answer/id #uuid "8dbc9696-4140-550a-b72f-debd09d5fe0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-351e7f30-9491-58fc-8cf5-793274bcf323" :answer/id #uuid "351e7f30-9491-58fc-8cf5-793274bcf323" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8aa4dff-702b-54c5-9a1b-1549247cc0ac" :answer/id #uuid "f8aa4dff-702b-54c5-9a1b-1549247cc0ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36ed8b6e-c128-5d01-928b-08f774f8d809" :answer/id #uuid "36ed8b6e-c128-5d01-928b-08f774f8d809" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e84734a-f76d-51fa-a6d7-e244eb448774" :answer/id #uuid "7e84734a-f76d-51fa-a6d7-e244eb448774" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116498 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the inverse of the matrix \n\n![](images/fd/fd9e9dc44e27810ef490a898a7c82ae9296eaf85ac9406cf310afe86638f71e1.svg) \n\nWe can compute the inverse of $A$ using elementary row operations. There are two key advantages to using row operations to compute the inverse of a matrix:\n\n- The method works for any invertible matrix of any size, not just $2\\times 2$ matrices.\n\n- It requires significantly less computation than some alternative methods. This is especially true for large matrices.\n\nHere, we'll focus on the $2\\times 2$ case. We use the following fact:\n\n*The row operations that transform $A$ into the identity matrix $I_2$ also transform $I_2$ into $A^{-1}$*.\n\nSo, to find the inverse of $A,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1:** Write down $\\color{blue}A$ and the identity matrix $I_2$ side-by-side, as shown below:\n![](images/e5/e553bcc265698432452c8d35b79fe9dc70c25010a0bcc6874ad0302ea7033ed7.svg)\n\n**Step 2:** Apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,{I}_{2}\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\,I\\,|\\,{A}^{-1}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n![](images/b7/b7c08e9a28ebfe456a9d77ccdaf6688893dfd04ba755a989a417c8a43e80ee0c.svg)\n\nWe can easily check our result by computing $AA^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115474 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
